00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
You've heard about orthogonization, how to set up your depth and test sets, human level

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.760
performance as a proxy for Bayes' era, and how to estimate your avoidable bias and variance.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.100
Let's pull it all together into a set of guidelines for how to improve the performance

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.800
of your learning algorithm.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:22.980
So I think getting a supervised learning algorithm to work well means fundamentally

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.920
hoping or assuming that you can do two things.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.280
One is that you can fit the training set pretty well.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:38.840
And you can think of this as roughly saying that you can achieve low avoidable bias.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:44.060
And the second thing you're assuming can do well is that doing well in a training set generalizes

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.800
pretty well to the depth set or the test set.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.920
And this is sort of saying that variance is not too bad.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.200
And in the spirit of orthogonization, what you see is that there's a certain set of

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:01.600
knobs you could use to fix avoidable bias issues, such as training a bigger network or

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.680
training longer.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:11.100
And there's a separate set of things you could use to address variance problems, such as regularization

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.580
or getting more training data.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:18.800
So to summarize the process we've seen in the last several videos, if you want to improve

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.920
the performance of your machine learning system, I would recommend looking at the difference

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.200
between your training error and your proxy for Bayes error.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.960
And this gives you a sense of the avoidable bias.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.680
In other words, just how much better do you think you should be trying to do on your

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.480
training set?

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.360
And then look at the difference between your depth error and your training error as an

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.120
estimate of how much of a variance problem you have.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:48.160
In other words, how much harder you should be working to make your performance generalized

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:58.140
from the training set to the depth set that it wasn't trained on explicitly.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:05.220
So to whatever extent you want to try to reduce avoidable bias, I would try to apply tactics

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:08.100
like train a bigger model.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.740
So you can just do better on your training sets or train longer, use a better optimization

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:28.980
algorithm, such as adds momentum or RMS prop or use a better algorithm like Adam.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:34.900
Or one other thing you could try is to just find a better neural network architecture or

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.500
better set of hyperparameters.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.300
And this could include everything from changing the activation function to changing the number

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.500
of layers or hidden units.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:49.340
Although if you do that, it would be in the direction of increasing the model size to

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:56.300
trying out other models or other model architectures, such as recurrent neural networks and convolutional

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.700
neural networks, which we'll see in later courses.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.420
Whether or not a new neural network architecture will fit your training set better is sometimes

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:09.700
hard to tell in advance, but sometimes you can get much better results with a better

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:10.700
architecture.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:16.500
Next to the extent that you find out variance is a problem, some of the many of techniques

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.860
you could try then includes the following.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:24.980
You can try to get more data because getting more data to train on could help you generalize

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:29.620
better to depth set data that you're out from data and see.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.780
You could try regularization.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:41.860
So this includes things like L2 regularization or dropout or data augmentation, which we talked

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.220
about in the previous course.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:50.220
Or once again, you can also try various neural network architecture hyperparameters search to

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.980
see if that can help you find a neural network architecture that is better suited for your

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:56.700
problem.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.660
I think that this notion of bias or avoidable bias and variance is one of those things

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.660
that's easier to learn but hard to master.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:09.180
And if you're able to systematically apply the concepts from this week's videos, you

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:14.740
actually be much more efficient and much more systematic and much more strategic than a

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:20.060
lot of machine learning teams in terms of how to systematically go about improving

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:22.900
the performance of your machine learning system.

